a number called gen_grid is 0.

a number called gen_whiles is 0.

a number called gen_repeats is 0.

a number called gen_grid_depth is 3.

(Whether THIS program will actually use its grid sink and its flag
 reader. Josj, reading forty programs: "I see good function definitions,
 but no call sites." He was right, and the count was damning — the grid
 sink was DEFINED in 40 of 40 programs and CALLED in 8, the flag reader
 defined in 40 and called in 10. So four fifths of programs carried a
 function nobody ever called: dead weight that pads the source, dilutes
 everything else a reader sees, and proves nothing about the compiler
 beyond its willingness to parse an unused definition.

 Both are now decided once per program, before any block is emitted, and
 the definition is emitted only when the program commits to calling it.
 The statement stream reads the same decision, so a program that defines
 the sink is guaranteed to reach for it.)

a boolean called gen_uses_grid is true.

(Call counters. Committing to emit a definition does not by itself
 produce a CALL — the leaf that calls it has to win the kind draw, which
 is why 22 of 40 programs still defined an uncalled grid sink after the
 first attempt at this. 'gen program' checks these after the statement
 budget is spent and emits one call if none happened, so a definition
 always has a call site.)

a number called gen_grid_calls is 0.

(Chained expansion, the grid: 0.4.5's Cartesian-product call form, joined
 by 'and' across two 'each ... from ...' clauses - a list clause and a
 range clause, over a two-number-parameter callee. The loop variables get
 their own e{N} names from a dedicated counter so two grid calls in the
 same program never bind the same identifier twice, which the one
 identifier space rule would otherwise make a shadowing question rather
 than a clean fresh name. The callee has no side effect worth printing,
 so this is a bare call statement - language note 3's "plain call
 statement" form, the same one the grid section itself uses when a fixed
 argument needs to come first.

 A grid clause yields NUMBERS and fills one parameter each, so the callee
 must take exactly two numbers. The prelude used to guarantee that with
 f3; nothing in the language does, so this asks the manifest and falls
 back to an ordinary call when this program drew no such function.)

To 'gen call grid' with a text called indent and a boolean called nested.
    a boolean called 'a callee is available' is 'gen manifest pick a numeric function from' of 2.
    If 'a callee is available' is false then,
        'gen call any function' of indent and nested,
        Return a boolean, false.
    a text called 'the callee' is 'gen function written' of gen_pick_function.
    (As many clauses as the callee has parameters, never fewer than two.
     The old leaf wrote exactly two because the prelude's f3 had exactly
     two; with the callee drawn, the clause count follows its arity the
     same way an ordinary call's argument count does. Written as a list
     clause and then range clauses, so the two clause forms the leaf
     exists to chain are both present whatever the arity.)
    a number called 'the arity' is element gen_pick_function of gen_function_arity.
    Set gen_grid to gen_grid add 1.
    a number called 'first loop name' is gen_grid.
    a number called 'list element one' is 'rng below' of 100.
    a number called 'list element two' is 'rng below' of 100.
    a text called clauses is "each e{'first loop name'} from [{'list element one'}, {'list element two'}]".
    a number called 'the clause' is 1.
    While 'the clause' is less than 'the arity',
        Set gen_grid to gen_grid add 1,
        a number called 'the loop name' is gen_grid,
        a number called 'range start' is 'rng below' of 5 add 1,
        a number called 'range spread' is 'rng below' of 4,
        a number called 'range end' is 'range start' add 'range spread',
        Set clauses to "{clauses} and each e{'the loop name'} from {'range start'} to {'range end'}",
        Set 'the clause' to 'the clause' add 1.
    a text called 'call line' is "{'the callee'} of {clauses}".
    a list called 'the lines' is ['call line'].
    'gen join lines' of indent and nested and 'the lines'.

(Deep grid expansion. Josj, 2026-08-20: "instead of double nested
 parameter loop expansions I wanna see 17 levels of nested parameter
 loop expansion." A grid clause binds to a PARAMETER - `f of each a from
 X and each b from Y` needs f to take two - so an N-deep grid requires
 an N-parameter callee, which this leaf emits alongside the call. The
 prelude's f1/f2/f3 top out at two parameters, which is precisely why
 the generator could never go deeper.

 Runtime stays bounded while the COMPILER does not: most clauses get a
 one-value range (`from i to i`), so the Cartesian product stays small
 no matter how deep the nesting goes, while codegen still has to build
 and close N nested loops. That is the intended asymmetry - depth
 stresses the compiler, and the product is what would stress only the
 clock. A handful of two-value ranges keep the product genuinely
 multi-iteration without letting it explode: worst case here is 2^3.

 Verified by hand at depth 17 before this leaf was written: compiles,
 runs, and sums correctly. Deeper is reachable by raising the cap.)

To 'gen leaf deep grid' with a text called indent and a boolean called nested.
    (A program that did not commit to a grid sink has no such function to
     call, so this leaf stands aside for one that always works. The
     decision is made once per program in 'gen program', which is what
     keeps definition and call site in step.)
    If gen_uses_grid is false then,
        'gen call any function' of indent and nested,
        Return a boolean, false.
    Set gen_grid to gen_grid add 1.
    a number called 'the run' is gen_grid.
    a number called depth is gen_grid_depth.
    a text called clauses is "".
    a number called 'spread budget' is 3.
    a number called i is 1.
    While i is less than depth add 1,
        a number called low is 'rng below' of 9,
        a number called high is low,
        a number called widen is 'rng below' of 4,
        If widen is 0 and 'spread budget' is positive then,
            Set high to low add 1,
            Set 'spread budget' to 'spread budget' minus 1.
        If i is 1 then,
            Set clauses to "each q{'the run'}s{i} from {low} to {high}".
        If i is greater than 1 then,
            Set clauses to "{clauses} and each q{'the run'}s{i} from {low} to {high}".
        Set i to i add 1.
    Set gen_grid_calls to gen_grid_calls add 1.
    a text called 'call line' is "{gen_grid_sink_name} of {clauses}".
    a list called 'the lines' is ['call line'].
    'gen join lines' of indent and nested and 'the lines'.

(`but if` chains (bugs #3 and #14 both lived here): a base action plus
 1-3 alternative branches, checked in order, first match wins. Two
 shapes, because 0.4.5's `otherwise` is hand-verified against the pinned
 binary to compile after `append`'s chain but NOT after `print`'s -
 `Print x, but if ... print y,
 otherwise print z.` is rejected ("Expected a statement, got Otherwise")
 under every punctuation variant tried, print-each included; the EBNF's
 own print_stmt production has no otherwise arm either, so the compiler
 is being consistent with its own grammar even though the prose
 describes otherwise as available to both. Print's chain therefore never
 carries one; append's always does, which is how "with and without a
 trailing otherwise" both get covered without emitting the illegal form.
 Hand-verified safe as a block's mandatory closer: unlike For-each/
 While/Repeat, a but-if chain's own trailing period does not leave
 anything open behind it (a for-each-in-while sole-body probe traps the
 next statement two-for-one; the equivalent but-if-chain probe does
 not), so both land in 'gen any leaf's pool, no remap needed.)

To 'gen leaf butif print' with a text called indent and a boolean called nested.
    a text called 'the base value' is 'gen expr'.
    a text called 'the chain' is "Print {'the base value'}".
    a number called 'the branch count' is 'rng below' of 3 add 1.
    a number called 'the branch index' is 1.
    While 'the branch index' is less than or equal to 'the branch count',
        a text called 'the condition' is 'gen condition',
        a text called 'the alternative' is 'gen expr',
        Set 'the chain' to "{'the chain'}, but if {'the condition'} print {'the alternative'}",
        Set 'the branch index' to 'the branch index' add 1.
    a list called 'the lines' is ['the chain'].
    'gen join lines' of indent and nested and 'the lines'.

To 'gen leaf butif append' with a text called indent and a boolean called nested.
    Set gen_lists to gen_lists add 1.
    a text called decl is "a list called l{gen_lists} is []".
    a text called 'the base value' is 'gen append expr'.
    a text called 'the chain' is "append {'the base value'} to l{gen_lists}".
    a number called 'the branch count' is 'rng below' of 3 add 1.
    a number called 'the branch index' is 1.
    While 'the branch index' is less than or equal to 'the branch count',
        a text called 'the condition' is 'gen condition',
        a text called 'the alternative' is 'gen append expr',
        Set 'the chain' to "{'the chain'}, but if {'the condition'} append {'the alternative'}",
        Set 'the branch index' to 'the branch index' add 1.
    a text called 'the otherwise value' is 'gen append expr'.
    Set 'the chain' to "{'the chain'}, otherwise append {'the otherwise value'}".
    a list called 'the lines' is [decl, 'the chain'].
    'gen join lines' of indent and nested and 'the lines'.

(`treating X as Y`: per-clause substitution on loop expansion. The
 print-each variant sometimes draws its collection from arguments's all
 (LANGUAGE.md's own canonical example). The brief's own item 5 flags
 that arguments's positional properties (first/second/last) need real
 arguments passed to be tested meaningfully, which this generator does
 not have - but arguments's all does not need that: it is a legal,
 always-empty collection with no arguments passed, so the treating
 clause parses and runs zero times, the same defined "does nothing"
 behaviour LANGUAGE.md documents for any empty collection.
 The grid variant chains two `each` clauses, each with its own treating,
 through f3 (language note 3's zero/one/two-arg prelude), reusing the
 grid's own e{N} loop-variable counter so two grid calls in one program
 never collide. Hand-verified UNSAFE as a block's mandatory closer -
 loop expansion is a nesting clause exactly like For-each's own key/
 element iteration (kinds 9/14) and the grid's `each ... and each ...`
 (kind 6's grid quarter) - so both remap to 1 under avoid-onerror and
 stay out of 'gen any leaf', same rule, same reason.)

To 'gen leaf treating print' with a text called indent and a boolean called nested.
    a number called 'source choice' is 'rng below' of 2.
    a text called source is "".
    a text called 'match value' is "".
    a text called 'replacement value' is "".
    If 'source choice' is 0 then,
        (arguments's all yields text elements (argv strings) - the
         treating match/replacement must be text too, or nasm rejects
         the emitted assembly with 'symbol _str_eq not defined'
         (hand-reduced: `print each item from arguments's all treating
         98 as 31.` alone reproduces it) - a number literal here compiles
         past the analyzer but the codegen path for a text-typed
         collection expects a text-typed match, and the mismatch never
         gets caught until the assembler.)
        Set source to "arguments's all",
        Set 'match value' to "\"-\"",
        Set 'replacement value' to "\"none\"".
    If 'source choice' is 1 then,
        a number called 'elem one' is 'rng below' of 100,
        a number called 'elem two' is 'rng below' of 100,
        a number called 'match number' is 'rng below' of 100,
        a number called 'replacement number' is 'rng below' of 100,
        Set source to "[{'elem one'}, {'elem two'}]",
        Set 'match value' to "{'match number'}",
        Set 'replacement value' to "{'replacement number'}".
    a text called line is "print each item from {source} treating {'match value'} as {'replacement value'}".
    a list called 'the lines' is [line].
    'gen join lines' of indent and nested and 'the lines'.

