(`While` and `Repeat`: today only `For each` is emitted. Both are
 self-contained, single-statement leaves with a hardcoded, unconditional
 `Increment`, never a body drawn from the recursive statement generator.

 The real defect this sidesteps. An earlier draft of this comment
 misdiagnosed it as While-specific; direct reproduction traced it to
 its actual root and filed it as bug #27: **a period never closes a
 Repeat body, in any nesting, at
 any depth - only a blank line does.** `Repeat 2 times, Print "r".`
 followed by `Print "after".`, with no blank line between them, prints
 `r after r after` - "after" is silently absorbed into the loop and
 re-runs with it, not a parse error, not a crash, just a program that
 means something other than what it reads as. `While` and `For each`
 both close correctly on a period, including a period stacked to close
 two levels at once (a For-each nested inside a While, closed with two
 periods on its own last statement, correctly closes both and the
 following statement runs once) - hand-verified, and it is what
 isolates the defect to Repeat specifically rather than "loops in
 general" or "While as the outer level," both of which an earlier
 version of this comment wrongly concluded from a Repeat-nested-in-
 While probe whose double-period failure was actually bug #27 wearing
 a While-shaped disguise.

 Consequence for this generator: 'gen leaf repeat' cannot just emit its
 closing `Print` as the next period-terminated line the way 'gen leaf
 while' does - that line would silently become part of the Repeat's own
 body (verified: it printed the counter once per iteration instead of
 once after, exactly bug #27's signature). It emits a blank line after
 the Repeat statement instead, which is what LANGUAGE.md documents as
 the only thing that closes a Repeat, before the closing `Print`.
 'gen leaf while' needs no such treatment - its own closing `Print`
 already closes correctly on its ordinary period, per the hand-
 verification above, so it is written the simpler way.

 Both stay reachable only at the true top level regardless (kind range
 widened at depth 3 only, left out of depth 2/1/0's ranges and out of
 'gen any leaf'): a blank line closes every open clause at once,
 including an enclosing function definition, so emitting one from
 inside a nested body would be unsafe for reasons that have nothing to
 do with bug #27, and 'gen leaf while' has no need to be reachable
 there either, so both are held to the same, simplest-to-verify
 position. Top level is also where 'nested' is always false, so every
 rendered line is independently period-closed to begin with - the
 blank line in 'gen leaf repeat' is closing the Repeat, not doing any
 work a period was already doing.)

To 'gen leaf while' with a text called indent and a boolean called nested.
    Set gen_whiles to gen_whiles add 1.
    a number called 'the limit' is 'rng below' of 8 add 1.
    a text called decl is "a number called u{gen_whiles} is 0".
    a text called 'loop line' is "While u{gen_whiles} is less than {'the limit'}, Increment u{gen_whiles}".
    a text called 'read line' is "Print u{gen_whiles}".
    a list called 'the lines' is [decl, 'loop line', 'read line'].
    'gen join lines' of indent and nested and 'the lines'.

(Bug #27 (see above): the Repeat statement's own period never closes
 it, so the closing `Print` is emitted after a blank line - the one
 thing that does close a Repeat - instead of joined onto 'the lines'
 the way every other leaf's closing read is.)

To 'gen leaf repeat' with a text called indent and a boolean called nested.
    Set gen_repeats to gen_repeats add 1.
    a number called 'the trips' is 'rng below' of 8 add 1.
    a text called decl is "a number called r{gen_repeats} is 0".
    a text called 'loop line' is "Repeat {'the trips'} times, Increment r{gen_repeats}".
    a list called 'the opening lines' is [decl, 'loop line'].
    'gen join lines' of indent and nested and 'the opening lines'.
    'gen emit' of "\n".
    a text called 'read line' is "Print r{gen_repeats}".
    a list called 'the closing lines' is ['read line'].
    'gen join lines' of indent and nested and 'the closing lines'.

(D4: `Break`/`Continue` - the one construct the generator never emitted
 (0 of 40 sampled programs). LANGUAGE.md "Loop Control" documents both
 as standalone statements: `Break.` exits the loop, `Continue.` skips to
 the next iteration. This leaf emits a bounded loop containing a guarded
 one of the four safe combinations, each hand-verified against v0.4.5
 (compiled with $VOX and run under `timeout`, the output confirmed to
 match the expected break/continue semantics):

   For each + Break
     For each number from 1 to 5,
         Print number,
         If number is equal to 3 then,
             Break.
         Print "after".
     -> 1 after 2 after 3 done  (Break skips the trailing Print at 3 and
         exits the loop; the range still bounds it - a guard value above
         5 just runs the full range with no break.)

   For each + Continue
     For each number from 1 to 5,
         Print number,
         If number is equal to 3 then,
             Continue.
         Print "after".
     -> 1 after 2 after 3 4 after 5 after done  (Continue skips only the
         trailing Print at 3; the For-each counter auto-advances, so the
         loop still terminates even though Continue skipped the body's
         rest - this is why For-each + Continue is safe without an
         explicit Increment.)

   While + Break
     a number called u1 is 0.
     While u1 is less than 5,
         Print u1,
         If u1 is equal to 3 then,
             Break.
         Increment u1.
     -> 0 1 2 3 done  (Break exits; Increment sits AFTER the If, so a
         break correctly skips it - we are leaving. The While condition
         bounds it regardless: a guard value >= 5 never fires and u1
         climbs to 5 and stops.)

   While + Continue
     a number called u1 is 0.
     While u1 is less than 5,
         Increment u1,
         If u1 is equal to 3 then,
             Continue.
         Print u1.
     -> 1 2 4 5 done  (Continue skips the Print at 3; Increment sits
         BEFORE the Continue, so progress is guaranteed even when the
         guard fires - the one ordering constraint 3 requires, and the
         one an unconstrained While + Continue hung on in an earlier
         probe: Continue before Increment advances nothing, the guard
         fires, the loop body repeats forever at the same counter.)

 The verified compilable shape (constraint 2): the loop head, body
 statement, If-guard and Break/Continue all live on one comma-joined
 line, the Break/Continue carrying its own period to close the If, and
 the loop's own closing period (appended by 'gen join lines' on its last
 line) terminating the whole statement - exactly the shape 'gen leaf
 while' already uses and 'gen leaf repeat' deliberately avoids. The
 trailing `Print "x"` / `Print u{N}` inside the body is what makes the
 break/continue observable in the program's output (fewer markers than
 the range when a Break fires, a gap in the markers when a Continue
 fires). The post-loop `Print u{N}` on the While forms is 'gen leaf
 while's own read-back, kept for the same reason: reaching it at all
 proves the loop terminated rather than hung.

 The guard value is drawn from a range that SOMETIMES exceeds the loop
 bound, so across seeds the guard fires for some programs and not for
 others - the honest demonstration that termination never depends on it
 firing, since the loop's own bound (For-each range / While condition)
 always closes the loop whether the guard was ever true or not.

 Safe-closer decision: this leaf is loop-bearing (For-each and While are
 both nesting clauses, rule 1), so its closing period closes the LOOP,
 not an enclosing block - exactly the reason kinds 9/14/6-grid and
 25-28 are excluded from 'gen any leaf'. It therefore does NOT join 'gen
 any leaf', and follows kinds 29/30 (While/Repeat) in being reachable
 only at the true top level: the While forms inherit 'gen leaf while's
 top-level-only position (their termination guarantee is that of an
 independent, self-closing top-level sentence), and the For-each forms
 are held there too for the same simplest-to-verify reason 'gen leaf
 while' gives. So kind 31 is added to the depth-3 draw only, left out of
 depth 2/1/0 and out of 'gen any leaf', and needs no avoid-onerror remap
 (at depth 3 avoid_onerror is false, the same reason 29/30 need none).
 'nested' is accordingly always false here, and the rendered lines are
 independently period-closed, the way 'gen leaf while's are.

 Fresh locals: the While forms declare a u{N} counter, reusing
 gen_whiles (the same counter 'gen leaf while' uses) so two While-bearing
 leaves in one program never collide on a u-name. The For-each forms use
 the built-in `number` loop variable and declare nothing fresh. Each of
 the four combinations lives in its own helper (the 'gen leaf call' ->
 'gen call zero/one/two/grid' pattern) so each has its locals at its own
 function top level - never inside an If branch, which keeps them
 permanently in scope and avoids both note 6b (a period inside a branch
 closing the branch before a later reference) and the same-name-across-
 branches collision 'gen leaf environment inrange' documented.)

To 'gen leaf loop control' with a text called indent and a boolean called nested.
    (The combo is drawn through 'rng below' of 13 then folded mod 4, NOT
     'rng below' of 4, deliberately. 'rng below' of N is r modulo N, and
     this generator's LCG has correlated low bits: the kind draw just
     before this ('rng below' of 32, in 'gen statement') returns 31 only
     when that r's low 5 bits are 11111, which forces the NEXT r to be
     divisible by 32 and therefore by 4 - so an immediately-following
     'rng below' of 4 (or of any multiple of 4) would ALWAYS return 0,
     locking the combo to 0 (Break-foreach) and making Continue
     unreachable for every seed. Hand-confirmed: with 'rng below' of 4
     here, 200 seeded programs produced 13 Break-foreach hits and zero
     Continue. 13 is prime and not a multiple of 4, so r modulo 13
     depends on the whole r, not just the low bits the LCG locks - the
     combo then varies across seeds and all four combinations are
     reachable. 13 modulo 4 maps 0..12 onto 0,1,2,3 as 4,3,3,3, a mild
     bias toward combo 0 that is irrelevant next to the coverage gain.)
    a number called 'the combo' is 'rng below' of 13.
    Set 'the combo' to 'the combo' modulo 4.
    If 'the combo' is 0 then,
        'gen leaf loop break foreach' of indent and nested.
    If 'the combo' is 1 then,
        'gen leaf loop continue foreach' of indent and nested.
    If 'the combo' is 2 then,
        'gen leaf loop break while' of indent and nested.
    If 'the combo' is 3 then,
        'gen leaf loop continue while' of indent and nested.

