(Not a compiler defect, and not something 'gen if' itself needs to work
 around - recorded here only because it explains why 'gen any leaf'
 refuses every For-each-bearing kind the guaranteed-closer role (see
 that function's own comment). LANGUAGE.md rule 1: a period closes only
 the innermost currently open clause. If a `For each` (block or
 single-line) is the body of a single-branch If, the For-each's own
 period closes the FOR EACH, not the If - the If is still open one
 level further out than the indentation suggests. Whatever textually
 "follows" the If at this point is therefore still inside its body, and
 it is THAT statement's period which finally closes the If. A
 declaration positioned there is accordingly "declared in only some
 branches" (there is no Otherwise), so vox correctly refuses it outside
 - not a defect, the documented branch-scoping rule doing exactly what
 it says. This only bites when the mandatory closer (see 'gen body')
 is itself a For-each-bearing leaf, since a plain leaf's period closes
 the If as intended; excluding those kinds from 'gen any leaf' is the
 whole fix, and a bare Otherwise-less If stays legal, generatable Vox.)

To 'gen if' with a number called depth and a text called indent.
    a text called 'the left operand' is 'gen var ref'.
    a text called 'the right operand' is 'gen var ref'.
    a text called head is "{indent}If {'the left operand'} is greater than {'the right operand'} then,\n".
    'gen emit' of head.
    a number called 'next depth' is depth minus 1.
    a text called 'next indent' is "{indent}  ".
    'gen body' of 'next depth' and 'next indent'.

(One callee per supported grid depth, emitted once in the prelude
 rather than beside the call site. A function definition is only legal
 at the top level, and 'gen leaf deep grid' can be selected inside a
 loop or If body, where emitting a `To ...` would be invalid Vox - the
 invariant forbids that, so the definitions live here where their
 position is fixed and legal by construction.)

To 'gen emit prelude grid sinks'.
    If gen_uses_grid is false then,
        Return a number, 0.
    (ONE callee, at the depth this program actually uses. The first
     version emitted all fifteen (depths 3..17) into every program
     whether or not any was called - measured cost: the shared prelude
     grew from 25 lines to 65, most of it dead definitions, which is
     boilerplate wearing coverage's coat. gen_grid_depth is drawn once
     per program in 'gen program' before any prelude is emitted, so the
     definition is top-level and legal by construction and the deep-grid
     leaf calls a sink that exists. Depth variety now lives ACROSS seeds
     instead of inside one program's dead code.)
    (Name and parameter names come off the manifest like any other
     function's, so `'grid sink 12'`, `p1..pN` and `gtotal` - which the
     invariant report found in 70% of programs - are gone with the rest
     of the template.)
    a text called 'the first parameter' is element 1 of gen_grid_sink_params.
    a text called 'the parameter list' is "a number called {'the first parameter'}".
    a text called sum is 'the first parameter'.
    a number called i is 2.
    While i is less than gen_grid_depth add 1,
        a text called 'the parameter' is element i of gen_grid_sink_params,
        Set 'the parameter list' to "{'the parameter list'} and a number called {'the parameter'}",
        Set sum to "{sum} add {'the parameter'}",
        Set i to i add 1.
    a text called 'the introducer' is 'gen parameter introducer'.
    a text called 'the definition' is "To {gen_grid_sink_name} {'the introducer'} {'the parameter list'}.\n    a number called {gen_grid_sink_local} is {sum}.\n    Print {gen_grid_sink_local}.\n\n".
    'gen emit' of 'the definition'.

(--- The ledger's control-flow leaves, batch A -------------------------

 docs/ledger/control-flow.md maps LANGUAGE.md:2203-2371 (Vox 0.4.15 pin;
 the section opens two lines earlier at the "## Control Flow" heading -
 cited here just past it, at the first body line, so a mechanical
 citation checker does not flag a bare heading)
 into numbered claims; the five leaves below put FLW-05, FLW-07, FLW-21,
 FLW-28 and FLW-41 on trial and ASSERT the documented result, the same
 way gen_buffers.vox's ledger leaves do - reusing 'gen assert number is'
 from there rather than copying it, per docs/ledger/PROCEDURE.md §6a
 ("assertion helpers... are plain text helpers despite their names; call
 them from any surface"). A failing assertion prints
 `ASSERT FLW-NN: expected X got Y` and exits 95, which loop_gen.vox
 classifies as a wrong-value finding carrying that line as its detail.

 All five are drawn TOP-LEVEL ONLY (kinds 200-204, folded onto depth 3's
 widened draw in 'gen statement' - see that function's own comment),
 never offered to 'gen any leaf' or to the depth 2/1/0 draws, the same
 position 'gen leaf while'/'gen leaf repeat'/'gen leaf loop control'
 hold. Punctuation is the subject of every one of these rows - a comma
 continuing a branch, a period closing one level, two periods closing
 two, a period continuing an if-chain across a newline - and 'gen join
 lines' only ever adds ONE period to a rendered line, and only to the
 LAST line of a call, regardless of nesting. Nested (comma-joined) use
 would corrupt at least three of the five: FLW-05 embeds an Otherwise
 inside one rendered line the same way 'gen leaf rich condition' (kind
 28) does, which LANGUAGE.md's "closing more than one level" section
 documents as unsafe once nested inside another still-open If (the
 Otherwise binds to the OUTER If once the inner one's own embedded
 period closes it - see kind 28's exclusion from 'gen any leaf' for the
 same hazard); FLW-07 needs an ACTUAL top-level newline between the If's
 period and Otherwise, which a comma-joined nested rendering would
 replace with a comma, changing which clause the comma even continues;
 FLW-21's list-form For-each is itself a nesting clause exactly like
 kinds 9/14/6-grid, so a comma after its own body action (nested=true)
 would silently fold the leaf's own trailing assertion into the loop
 body instead of running it once afterward. Top-level only sidesteps all
 three at once, and FLW-28 and FLW-41 are held there too for the
 simplest-to-verify reason 'gen leaf while' already gives.

 Every claim below was hand-run against the pinned vox 0.4.14 before
 being written; the five retained probes are docs/ledger/probes/
 control-flow/FLW-05.vox, FLW-07.vox, FLW-21.vox, FLW-28.vox, FLW-41.vox.

 Witness/tally names come off gen_buffers.vox's own word pool
 ('gen buffer name' for an unused word) rather than a fixed phrase plus
 a counter (review round 2, master, 2026-08-29): the first version named
 every one of them "'the branch tally N'"/"'the lap tally N'"/etc., and
 the merged invariants diff caught `tally` alone crossing the report's
 50% line - a fixed word four of these five leaves all happened to
 share, cited by nothing in LANGUAGE.md. The pool already gives
 program-unique, readable names and already tracks what is taken per
 program (shared with every buffer in the corpus, so a flow witness and
 a real buffer never collide on a word either) - reusing it needs no
 counter of this surface's own, so gen_flow_branches/chains/empties/
 stacks are gone along with their resets in 'gen program'.

 'gen flow witness name' (below) draws the word and decides the WRITTEN
 form itself rather than calling 'gen buffer reference' for that part -
 hand-reduced while wiring this up: `Print "{'pantry'}"` - a single-
 quoted, single WORD name, no property after it - fails to compile
 ("Unknown variable: 'pantry'") even though `Print 'pantry'.` alone and
 `Print "{'pantry's size}"` (the same name WITH a property) both compile
 and run correctly (repro in REPORT-LEAVES-FLOW-A.md, not filed as a
 ledger discrepancy - that adjudication is the master's, not a leaf
 worker's). Every one of these five leaves' assertions interpolates the
 bare witness with no property to hide behind, and the pool quotes a
 one-word name on roughly half its draws (LANGUAGE.md:760 makes that
 quoting optional, not required) - so a naive `'gen buffer reference'`
 call would compile four leaves in five and fail the fifth at random,
 seed to seed. The fix quotes only when the drawn word actually has a
 space (the case LANGUAGE.md:760 requires it for, and the one case
 already proven safe to interpolate); a one-word draw is written bare.
 Recorded as a candidate in the batch report, not adjudicated here.)

To 'gen flow witness name'.
    a text called 'the stem' is 'gen buffer name'.
    a boolean called 'the stem is one word' is 'gen name is one word' of 'the stem'.
    If 'the stem is one word' then,
        Return a text, gen_buffer_last_word.
    a text called 'the reference' is 'gen buffer reference' of 'the stem'.
    Return a text, 'the reference'.

(FLW-05 (LANGUAGE.md:2218): "Separate multiple actions in a branch with
 commas" - the manual's own worked example (2211-2213) shows this only
 for the THEN branch; nothing shows the Otherwise arm doing the same,
 though the sentence-consumption rule (2207) draws no distinction
 between branches. Branch SELECTION is FLW-01/FLW-02's claim, already
 covered elsewhere, so this leaf forces the Otherwise arm to fire - the
 comparison operator and which operand carries the gap vary across three
 shapes (always false, never the same reason twice) so the corpus does
 not read "the condition is always false" as one fixed template. Each
 comma-joined action in the Otherwise arm shifts a running tally one
 decimal digit left and adds its own 1-based position, so the value read
 back afterwards is 12 for two actions, 12345 for five: it can only be
 that number if every action ran, exactly once each, in the order
 written - any action skipped, doubled, or reordered lands on a
 different number, so the single assertion covers completeness and
 ordering together. Hand-verified against vox 0.4.14 at docs/ledger/
 probes/control-flow/FLW-05.vox (3 comma actions in the Otherwise arm):
 the tally read back 123.)

To 'gen leaf if comma actions otherwise' with a text called indent and a boolean called nested.
    a text called 'the tally name' is 'gen flow witness name'.
    a number called 'the base value' is 'rng below' of 1000 add 1.
    a number called 'the gap' is 'rng below' of 50 add 1.
    a number called 'the operator choice' is 'rng below' of 3.
    a number called 'the left value' is 'the base value'.
    a number called 'the right value' is 'the base value'.
    a text called comparison is "is not equal to".
    If 'the operator choice' is 0 then,
        Set 'the right value' to 'the base value' add 'the gap',
        Set comparison to "is greater than".
    If 'the operator choice' is 1 then,
        Set 'the left value' to 'the base value' add 'the gap',
        Set comparison to "is less than".
    a text called 'the tally decl' is "a number called {'the tally name'} is 0".
    a text called condition is "{'the left value'} {comparison} {'the right value'}".
    a number called 'the action count' is 'rng below' of 4 add 2.
    a text called 'the otherwise chain' is "".
    a number called position is 1.
    While position is less than or equal to 'the action count',
        a text called action is "Set {'the tally name'} to {'the tally name'} multiply 10 add {position}",
        If position is 1 then,
            Set 'the otherwise chain' to action.
        If position is greater than 1 then,
            Set 'the otherwise chain' to "{'the otherwise chain'}, {action}".
        Set position to position add 1.
    a number called 'the expected tally' is 0.
    Set position to 1.
    While position is less than or equal to 'the action count',
        Set 'the expected tally' to 'the expected tally' multiply 10 add position,
        Set position to position add 1.
    a text called 'the if line' is "If {condition} then, Print \"unreachable\". Otherwise, {'the otherwise chain'}".
    a text called 'the assertion' is 'gen assert number is' of "FLW-05" and 'the tally name' and 'the expected tally'.
    a list called 'the lines' is ['the tally decl', 'the if line', 'the assertion'].
    'gen join lines' of indent and nested and 'the lines'.

(FLW-07 (LANGUAGE.md:2220): "A period before but if/otherwise is treated
 as part of the same if-chain when the chain continues" - hand-verified
 across an ACTUAL newline, the one dimension no existing leaf reaches:
 every other Otherwise in this generator sits on the SAME rendered line
 as its `then,` arm (one format-string element joined by 'gen join
 lines'), so the period before it never crosses real whitespace.
 Building the If and the Otherwise as TWO SEPARATE elements of 'the
 lines' does exactly that: 'gen join lines' terminates each with its own
 period and its own newline (top level only, so every line gets a
 period, not a comma), so the rendered program reads exactly like the
 manual's own worked example - the If on one line, a period, a newline,
 then Otherwise starting the next line - an ORDINARY newline, not a
 blank one (which would force-close something else instead). WHICH
 branch fires is genuinely random here (this row is about the
 punctuation, not the selection FLW-01/02 already cover): the generator
 draws both operands and a comparison and works out for itself, before
 rendering, which side will win, then asserts a witness that only
 matches if the side it predicted is the side that actually ran. Hand-
 verified against vox 0.4.14 at docs/ledger/probes/control-flow/
 FLW-07.vox.)

To 'gen leaf if otherwise across newline' with a text called indent and a boolean called nested.
    a text called 'the outcome name' is 'gen flow witness name'.
    a number called 'the left value' is 'rng below' of 100.
    a number called 'the right value' is 'rng below' of 100.
    a number called 'the operator choice' is 'rng below' of 3.
    a text called comparison is "is equal to".
    If 'the operator choice' is 0 then,
        Set comparison to "is greater than".
    If 'the operator choice' is 1 then,
        Set comparison to "is less than".
    a boolean called 'the then arm fires' is false.
    If 'the operator choice' is 0 and 'the left value' is greater than 'the right value' then,
        Set 'the then arm fires' to true.
    If 'the operator choice' is 1 and 'the left value' is less than 'the right value' then,
        Set 'the then arm fires' to true.
    If 'the operator choice' is 2 and 'the left value' is equal to 'the right value' then,
        Set 'the then arm fires' to true.
    a number called 'the expected outcome' is 2.
    If 'the then arm fires' then,
        Set 'the expected outcome' to 1.
    a text called 'the outcome decl' is "a number called {'the outcome name'} is 0".
    a text called condition is "{'the left value'} {comparison} {'the right value'}".
    a text called 'the if line' is "If {condition} then, Set {'the outcome name'} to 1".
    a text called 'the otherwise line' is "Otherwise, Set {'the outcome name'} to 2".
    a text called 'the assertion' is 'gen assert number is' of "FLW-07" and 'the outcome name' and 'the expected outcome'.
    a list called 'the lines' is ['the outcome decl', 'the if line', 'the otherwise line', 'the assertion'].
    'gen join lines' of indent and nested and 'the lines'.

(FLW-21 (LANGUAGE.md:2277, undocumented precision): the list form of For
 each over an EMPTY list runs the body zero times, no error - the manual
 states the list form (2264-2273) but never says what an empty list
 does. Hand-verified against vox 0.4.14 at docs/ledger/probes/control-
 flow/FLW-21.vox that it simply runs zero times: no crash, no error. The
 witness starts at a sentinel the body would overwrite if it ran even
 once, so reading it back unchanged after the loop is the whole
 assertion. The list itself is fresh every draw (gen_lists, shared with
 every other list-declaring leaf in the corpus, so two of them in one
 program never collide on a name); the loop variable ('item') is not,
 the same way every existing range-form leaf already reuses the bare
 name 'number' across separate top-level loops with no collision - a
 For-each's own loop variable is scoped to that loop, not the program.)

To 'gen leaf empty list loop' with a text called indent and a boolean called nested.
    Set gen_lists to gen_lists add 1.
    a text called 'the witness name' is 'gen flow witness name'.
    a text called 'the list decl' is "a list called l{gen_lists} is []".
    a text called 'the witness decl' is "a number called {'the witness name'} is 0".
    a text called 'the loop line' is "For each item in l{gen_lists}, Set {'the witness name'} to 1".
    a text called 'the assertion' is 'gen assert number is' of "FLW-21" and 'the witness name' and 0.
    a list called 'the lines' is ['the list decl', 'the witness decl', 'the loop line', 'the assertion'].
    'gen join lines' of indent and nested and 'the lines'.

(FLW-28 (LANGUAGE.md:2321-2331): periods stack - a Repeat nested inside
 a For-each takes two periods, on the same rendered line, to close both
 levels at once (see "Closing more than one level"). This is NOT the
 historical bug #27 shape documented above 'gen leaf repeat' (a LONE
 period failing to close a TOP-LEVEL Repeat, fixed in v0.4.6 per FLW-26
 and FUZZER_DEFECTS.md) - this Repeat is NESTED inside a For-each and
 closed by a period stacked with the For-each's own, exactly the shape
 LANGUAGE.md's own worked example uses, and gen_flow.vox's existing
 comment above 'gen leaf while' already records that stacked closes on a
 nested loop hand-verify correctly on 0.4.9. This leaf varies both
 counts (2-5 each) and asserts the PRODUCT via a running tally (proving
 every inner iteration of every outer iteration actually ran) and that
 the statement after the double period ran exactly once (proving the
 double period closed BOTH levels together - not zero, not two or more).
 Built as raw text via 'gen emit' rather than through 'gen join lines'
 for the two-period line specifically, because 'gen join lines' only
 ever appends ONE period, to the LAST line of a call: this construct
 needs two literal periods immediately adjacent on one line, wherever it
 sits in 'the lines'. Hand-verified against vox 0.4.14 at docs/ledger/
 probes/control-flow/FLW-28.vox (2 outer x 2 inner): the lap tally read
 back 4 and the after-tally read back 1.)

To 'gen leaf stacked periods' with a text called indent and a boolean called nested.
    a number called 'the outer count' is 'rng below' of 4 add 2.
    a number called 'the inner count' is 'rng below' of 4 add 2.
    a number called 'the expected product' is 'the outer count' multiply 'the inner count'.
    a text called 'the lap tally name' is 'gen flow witness name'.
    a text called 'the after tally name' is 'gen flow witness name'.
    a text called 'the lap decl' is "a number called {'the lap tally name'} is 0".
    a text called 'the after decl' is "a number called {'the after tally name'} is 0".
    a list called 'the setup lines' is ['the lap decl', 'the after decl'].
    'gen join lines' of indent and nested and 'the setup lines'.
    a text called 'the inner action' is "Set {'the lap tally name'} to {'the lap tally name'} add 1".
    a text called 'the outer head' is "{indent}For each lap from 1 to {'the outer count'},\n".
    a text called 'the inner line' is "{indent}  Repeat {'the inner count'} times, {'the inner action'}..\n".
    a text called block is "{'the outer head'}{'the inner line'}".
    'gen emit' of block.
    a text called 'the after action' is "Set {'the after tally name'} to {'the after tally name'} add 1".
    a text called 'the product assertion' is 'gen assert number is' of "FLW-28" and 'the lap tally name' and 'the expected product'.
    a text called 'the after assertion' is 'gen assert number is' of "FLW-28" and 'the after tally name' and 1.
    a list called 'the closing lines' is ['the after action', 'the product assertion', 'the after assertion'].
    'gen join lines' of indent and nested and 'the closing lines'.

(FLW-41 (LANGUAGE.md:2361): `terminate` is an alternative keyword for
 `Exit` and takes a code - already drawn generically as one of three
 spellings for the program's own NATURAL tail ('gen program ending' in
 gen_core.vox), but never before as an ordinary statement in the middle
 of the statement stream, which is the shape this leaf adds: wherever it
 lands, a witness line prints and the very next statement the program
 executes is `terminate <code>.`. src/loop_gen.vox's own classifier
 comment reads "A nonzero exit code is normal and is not a finding", and
 its exit-code handling only treats 91-94 (argv) and 95 (the ledger's
 own channel) specially - so a nonzero exit outside 90-99 is tolerated,
 not a finding, which is the SECOND of the two cases prompt.md names:
 the code is drawn at random from the full non-reserved range rather
 than pinned to 0 (the alternative it offers for a runner that WOULD
 treat a nonzero exit as a finding, which this one does not - see
 src/runner.vox and loop_gen.vox's classifier, read before this leaf was
 written). Nothing about it can be asserted from INSIDE the exiting
 program - there is no statement left to run once it has - so this is a
 harness-side claim like FLW-34/35/36/38/40: hand-verified against vox
 0.4.14 at docs/ledger/probes/control-flow/FLW-41.vox, where the witness
 line printed and the process's actual exit status was the code that
 followed it.)

To 'gen leaf terminate' with a text called indent and a boolean called nested.
    a number called 'the raw code' is 'rng below' of 246.
    a number called 'the exit code' is 'the raw code'.
    If 'the raw code' is greater than 89 then,
        Set 'the exit code' to 'the raw code' add 10.
    a text called keyword is "Terminate".
    a number called 'the case draw' is 'rng below' of 7.
    If 'the case draw' is less than 3 then,
        Set keyword to 'gen in lower case' of keyword.
    a text called witness is "Print \"reached the terminate statement\"".
    a text called ending is "{keyword} {'the exit code'}".
    a list called 'the lines' is [witness, ending].
    'gen join lines' of indent and nested and 'the lines'.
